{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/dao.stream.http.md docs/design/dao.star.md'","aggregated_output":"# DaoStream HTTP Boundary\n\nStatus: design, not implemented. Subordinate to\n[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which is the contract and wins on any\ndisagreement; sibling of [`dao.stream.ws.md`](./dao.stream.ws.md), whose\ndeposit model and admission rule it reuses. Every sentence below is a rule.\nThis document is application-agnostic: it specifies HTTP exchanges as\nevents and effects and nothing about what they carry.\n\n## What it is\n\nThe HTTP boundary is not a transport and owns no stream. It is an adapter\n(events in) and a host effect interpreter (effects out) over two ordinary\nmedia the composition supplies: an **ingress** medium it deposits into and\nan **egress** medium it reads. It has no reader surface, no `create!`, and\nhands no handle upward. It never reads a request body, a query string or a\nresponse chunk. Because nothing host-local crosses either medium, both are\n`:portable-values`, and the interpreter answering requests may sit on any\npeer (see *No privileged node*).\n\n## Exchange\n\nOne request and its response are one attachment; a connection is not\n(HTTP/1.1 keep-alive and HTTP/2 multiplexing are connection facts below\nthe transform). The boundary mints `:http/exchange` under the contract's\nserver-minted attachment rule (`dao.stream.md`, Envelopes): plain data,\nunique among the exchanges it can distinguish, and never reused for the\nlifetime of the egress medium. It carries the boundary's incarnation, so a\nstale effect retained from before a restart can never match a new socket.\n\n**Inventory.** The set of exchanges currently open is a resource the\nboundary possesses. It is not derivable from the ingress medium, which\nevicts. The boundary exposes it as one transport-owned operation beside\n`egress-step`, `open-exchanges`, answering the open ids, together with the\nidempotent `close-exchange`. Both exist for a `dao.lease.md` judge composed\ninside this boundary (reclaim procedure and restart inventory); code\nwritten against the contract may not reach for them.\n\n## Events in\n\nWire framing, header parsing and size limits happen below the transform;\nan over-limit or malformed request is answered there (400, 413, 503) and is\nnot an event. Every valid request is deposited unjudged. In these examples\nthe exchange id is `[incarnation n]` and strings stand for byte values:\n\n```clojure\n{:http/exchange [7 42]\n :http/event    :http/request\n :http/method   :post\n :http/path     \"/action\"\n :http/query    \"a=1\"\n :http/headers  {\"accept\" \"text/event-stream\"}\n :http/body     \"{\\\"count\\\":1}\"}\n\n{:http/exchange [7 42] :http/event :http/ended}\n{:http/exchange [7 42] :http/event :http/closed}\n```\n\n`:http/method` is a lower-case keyword; `:http/query` and `:http/body` are\nabsent when the request has none; header names are lower-cased.\n\nExactly one terminal event is deposited per exchange, after its request\nevent, whatever the cause: the peer closed, a write failed, an effect was\nout of order, the composition's judge reclaimed it, the boundary stopped.\n`:http/ended` is reserved for a response the boundary completed by\nperforming an `:http/end` effect. A terminal event reports that the\nexchange ended; it says nothing about which individual effects reached the\npeer.\n\n## Deposit admission\n\nAs `dao.stream.ws.md`, Deposit Admission. The constructor takes the\ningress destination and its admission declaration as data: evict-oldest\nretention with a declared capacity, value domain `:portable-values`, able\nto carry every envelope above, and a lifetime longer than the boundary.\nWiring a reject-mode destination is a host assembly defect refused at\nassembly. The constructor also takes `:http/max-open`, the number of\nexchanges held open at once, and `:http/max-body-bytes`; a request beyond\neither is answered below the transform and never deposited.\n\nEviction is loss. A reader whose cursor spans an ingress `gap` has missed\nrequest or terminal events and cannot reconstruct them from this medium;\nthe inventory above is where the possessing side's truth is.\n\n## Effects out\n\n`egress-step` is a step the composition drives at its own cadence, as it\ndrives `endpoint-step`. It holds one cursor on the egress medium, minted at\n`:dao.stream/newest` before the listener starts, and performs effects in\nthe egress medium's order:\n\n```clojure\n{:http/exchange [7 42] :http/effect :http/head\n :http/status 200 :http/headers {\"content-type\" \"text/plain\"}}\n{:http/exchange [7 42] :http/effect :http/chunk :http/body \"hello\"}\n{:http/exchange [7 42] :http/effect :http/end}\n```\n\n`:http/head` is sent once and first; `:http/chunk` writes bytes, a string\nas UTF-8, and flushes; `:http/end` completes the response and the\nexchange. The step appends no per-effect outcome, and a performed write is\nnot a delivery. A value that is not an effect for an exchange this boundary\nholds open is not this interpreter's and is ignored, whether it names\nnothing, names an exchange already ended or closed, or arrives after an\n`:http/end`: the egress medium has no privileged reader, and other\ninterpreters may share it. An effect out of order for an open exchange, a\nchunk before the head or a second head, closes that exchange.\n\nOrder is per medium. The ingress and egress media are two sequences, and\nno ordering holds between them; a writer that must know whether its effect\nwas performed asks a question the wire answers, by what the peer later\nsends, not this boundary.\n\nThe step takes no clock and holds no timer. How long a silent exchange is\nheld open is the composition's, through `dao.lease.md`; `close-exchange` is\nthe reclaim procedure a judge is composed with.\n\n## Observably gone\n\nA boundary that cannot deposit or cannot write makes itself observably\ngone by closing a resource it itself holds, the same action for every\ncause (`dao.stream.md`, Surfaces):\n\n- a non-`ok` ingress deposit closes that exchange's socket with no\n  synthesized response; the terminal event has nowhere to land, which is\n  the contract's irreducible corner;\n- a failed socket write closes the exchange and deposits `:http/closed`;\n- an egress `gap` closes every open exchange, since the step cannot say\n  whose effects were lost, then adopts the recovery cursor;\n- an egress `end` or `transport-error` closes every open exchange and the\n  listener: the boundary can never answer again.\n\nIt invokes nothing above itself, retries nothing, and interprets nothing.\n\nA host may report no failure for a write its buffers accepted, and may never\nreport a peer that disconnects while an exchange is idle (http-kit does both:\n`send!` answers true into a dead channel and `on-close` does not fire). A\nperformed write is still not a delivery; detection then belongs to the\ncomposition's lease — a silent exchange lapses and `close-exchange` reclaims\nit — with latency bounded by the exchange lease's duration plus the judge's\ncadence (architect ruling 2026-10-24,\n`collab/1792840534057-architect-dao-star-m1-client-close`).\n\n## No privileged node\n\nWhich end accepted is an establishment fact: HTTP has a requester and a\nresponder because TCP has a dialer and an acceptor, and a browser can only\ndial (`dao.stream.remote.md`, section 1). The ingress and egress media are\nordinary streams, so a peer that cannot listen may run the answering\ninterpreter against reflections of them served by a reachable peer that\nruns only this boundary. The boundary records no role beyond the exchange.\n\nHosts: clj (http-kit is already a dependency of the WebSocket transport),\ncljs on Node, cljd. A browser has no listener.\n\n## Deferred\n\n- An HTTP channel for `dao.stream.remote.md` (request maps by POST, answers\n  by a long-lived response) for peers that cannot open a WebSocket.\n- Request bodies as streams rather than one bounded value.\n- TLS termination, CORS and compression: below the transform, unspecified\n  here.\n# dao.star: Hypermedia Web Applications in datom.world\n\nStatus: design; Mode 1 ratified by owner ruling (2026-10-24). Mode 1 is\nspecified and buildable on [`dao.stream.http.md`](./dao.stream.http.md) and\n[`dao.lease.md`](./dao.lease.md).\nMode 2 is proposed and waits on fenced custody, version 1 of\n[`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md`](./yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md)\n(owner ruling 2026-10-07). The published framing is\n[`daostar-vs-datastar-vs-electric-clojure.blog`](../../public/chp/blog/daostar-vs-datastar-vs-electric-clojure.blog).\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\n`dao.star` is not a library or an adapter; it is the architectural pattern for building web applications natively in `datom.world`. It emerges from the intersection of two aligned philosophies:\n1. **datom.world**: Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data flowing over explicit, append-only streams (Axioms 1 and 2). It avoids compiler magic, instead exposing the network explicitly via data.\n2. **Datastar**: The browser should be a dumb, stateless interpreter of hypermedia (HTML) delivered over Server-Sent Events (SSE).\n\nDatastar interprets the SSE projection of an egress `dao.stream`. That is enough to eliminate frontend state management, reactive DAG compilers (like Electric Clojure), and proprietary RPC adapters: standard HTTP in, standard SSE out, and every piece in between is an ordinary stream or an ordinary interpreter.\n\n`dao.star` provides two operational modes under one programming model: **The Thin Client** (Arc-style sequential UI) and **The Fat Client** (mobile continuations).\n\n**Roles, not nodes.** \"Server\" below means the peer whose composition holds the HTTP boundary; \"browser\" means a peer that can only dial. Neither is a kind of node, and no protocol privilege attaches to either (`datom.world.md`, Design Principles). The HTTP boundary's two media are ordinary streams, so the agent answering requests may run on a peer other than the one that listens, over `dao.stream.remote.md` reflections.\n\n---\n\n## Mode 1: The Thin Client (Arc-Style Sequential UI)\n\nIn the thin client model the browser holds zero business logic and zero shared mutable state. Datastar acts as a dumb terminal rendering HTML patches, while `yin.vm` executes linear, sequential code on the server.\n\n### Flow\n\n1. **Events In**: A user clicks a button (`data-on-click=\"@post('/k/<token>')\"`). Datastar fires a standard HTTP request. The generic HTTP boundary (`dao.stream.http.md`) mints an exchange id, deposits the request as an unjudged `:http/request` event into the ingress `dao.stream` the composition wired, and holds the socket open. It reads nothing inside the request.\n2. **Routing**: A router step in the `dao.star` composition reads the ingress with its own cursor, classifies each request by its path (*Sessions and request kinds*), and forwards it to the medium of the session its token names, together with every later terminal event of that exchange. The boundary does not route.\n3. **Resuming**: The session's driver, on its next poll, observes the input at its own cursor and steps the parked continuation. Nothing wakes anything; a stream invokes nothing.\n4. **Semantics and State**: The `yin.vm` agent evaluates business logic linearly. The UI is a derivation of the continuation's state, never stored: the agent projects it into Datastar's SSE wire format (`event: datastar-patch-elements\\ndata: <div>...</div>`) through one pure projection function the composition supplies.\n5. **Effects Out**: The agent appends to the egress `dao.stream`, tagged with the exchange id: an `:http/head` effect (status, `Content-Type: text/event-stream`), one `:http/chunk` per SSE event with the fully constructed string, and `:http/end` when the response is complete. A subscription exchange simply never ends.\n6. **Dumb Socket Routing**: The boundary's `egress-step` observes the egress stream, matches the exchange id to the open socket, and flushes the unmodified payload. Datastar morphs the DOM. The agent parks again, waiting for the next input, for as long as it takes.\n\n### Sessions and request kinds\n\nA **lineage** is one session's chain of occurrences: a root and every successor a consumed input produced. **The token is the occurrence id**, minted unguessable by the driver; the lineage's identity is its root's id. Every projected UI carries the id of the occurrence that rendered it. The lineage's **current** occurrence is the one with no successor.\n\nThe driver appends one fact per transition to the **session-facts** medium, and appends it with `:dao.stream/ok` before any UI carrying the new id is projected:\n\n```clojure\n{:dao.star/lineage S :dao.star/occurrence S}                            ; root\n{:dao.star/lineage S :dao.star/occurrence O2 :dao.star/predecessor O1}  ; consumption\n{:dao.star/lineage S :dao.star/ended :halted}                           ; the program finished\n```\n\nThe router materializes occurrence → lineage → current from these facts and from the lease medium's `:lapsed` records (*Liveness*), as `dao.space.index` materializes a covered index, never from a table it keeps. The session-facts medium is a complete-history transport (`dao.stream.md`, Complete history); its growth is bounded only by OD-4's resolution, an open dependency.\n\n**Observation order.** A fact's `ok` is publication, not the router's observation of it, so the router never classifies from a projection that may lag. Each router pass reads the ingress first, then drains its session-facts and lineage-lease cursors to `blocked`, then classifies what it read. A token reaches the ingress only through a UI projected after its fact was accepted, so a fact precedes every request that names it, and a pass that has read the request has the fact to drain. The same holds for successor ids.\n\nThe drain proves this only on a direct handle: a reflection answers `blocked` while its request to the source is pending (`dao.stream.remote.md`, 2.4), which says nothing about the source. So **the router is composed on the peer that holds the session-facts medium, the lineage judge and the drivers**, and reads those two classification media directly; any other medium may be a reflection, which is the topology *Roles, not nodes* permits, so a driver separated from the HTTP boundary observes exchange grants and lapses over a reflection of the boundary's lease medium. A reflected ingress delays the read of a request, never its fact, so the proof stands. A router that read its facts through a reflection could not classify unknown at all; that composition is not permitted.\n\nThe router classifies by path; the boundary reads neither path nor token.\n\n| Path | Kind | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| `/` (no token) | begin | The root program starts a fresh lineage and answers a complete `text/html` document: `datastar.js`, actions carrying the root id, and `data-init=\"@get('/s/<token>')\"`. |\n| `/s/<token>` | subscribe | Opens the session's long-lived SSE exchange. Consumes nothing and advances nothing: the driver answers the head and a projection of the current occurrence, and every later patch goes here too. Reconnecting with the original URL is a new subscribe, resolved to the current occurrence however many inputs have passed. |\n| `/k/<token>`, token current | input | Consumed by the current occurrence (*Inputs, order and replay*). |\n| `/k/<token>`, token consumed | stale | Runs nothing. Answered by a projection of the current occurrence, then `:http/end`. |\n| any token of an ended or unknown lineage | begin | Halted, lapsed, or lost to a restart: answered as `/`. Unknown is judged only after the drain above. |\n\nSubscribe and stale requests are **re-projections**: the driver applies the projection function to the parked state without stepping it.\n\n### Signals\n\nA Datastar request carries the client's signals: in the `datastar` query parameter for GET and DELETE, in the JSON body otherwise. The boundary deposits them where HTTP put them, inside `:http/query` or `:http/body`, unread.\n\nDecoding is interpretation. `dao.star` supplies one pure projection from a request event to a signals map. The ingress tuple gains no slot for it, and signals become datoms only when an application interpreter derives them from the retained ingress stream (`datom.world.md`, Derive, don't persist).\n\nSignals are client-held state that moves, not shared state: each request carries an immutable snapshot, and the agent changes them only by appending a `datastar-patch-signals` effect. They are also the client's unverified claim. A signal is input to a continuation, never its state and never authority.\n\n### Inputs, order and replay\n\n**The token is the gate.** It is the request's only routing and idempotence key, and until ShiBi is load-bearing it is the sole gate on Mode 1 input: a pre-shared value in `dao.shibi.md`'s sense (owner ruling 2026-10-07).\n\n**Order is the ingress position.** Two inputs for one occurrence are not concurrent once deposited: one is earlier. Custody decides who consumes it. In a one-process composition the session's single driver is the only resumer; that exclusion is process-scoped and says so. Where more than one peer observes the ingress, resumption is a take, and a take is a lease on the occurrence (UCF §7.7).\n\n**One input, one successor.** Consuming an input advances occurrence `O` to its successor `O'` and appends the lineage fact. The reply goes to the exchange that carried the consumed input; patches for the session go to its open subscription exchanges, which the driver holds as its own interpreter state, built from the subscribe requests and terminal events routed to it.\n\n**Stale input is one-shot** (owner ruling 2026-10-07). A double click, a retry, a replayed URL, a second tab or the back button names an `O` that already has a successor. It runs nothing and is re-projected. The UI is a derivation, so answering twice is harmless, and a retry whose first reply was lost still converges. Mode 1 continuations are one-shot; multi-shot forks are deferred.\n\n**Effects.** Exactly-once needs a fenced writer into an enrolled consumer (UCF §7.7.5). Every other stream is at-least-once and declared so.\n\n### Liveness\n\nTwo leased resources, kept apart. Neither adds a clock to the wait for input: a parked continuation's `next` on its input is `blocked` until a request arrives, on a Tuesday or a Thursday.\n\n**The open exchange is leased.** The judge runs inside the peer that holds the HTTP boundary and is composed with the boundary's `close-exchange` as reclaim procedure and `open-exchanges` as its inventory (`dao.stream.http.md`, Inventory). For each `:http/request` it observes it grants unsolicited on a lease medium it writes: subject `{:http/exchange x}`, holder the egress medium's identity, a duration in `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`. Reclaim closes the exchange; the adapter deposits `:http/closed`, then the judge records `:lapsed`. A responder that keeps an exchange open observes its grant and appends `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal :dao.lease/lease L}` to the egress medium at under half the duration, from its own control flow, against its own tick stream. The judge and `egress-step` are peer observers of that medium; each ignores the other's facts. A one-shot responder answers inside the granted duration and renews nothing. An exchange nobody answers lapses by `:silence`; Datastar's reconnect opens a new one.\n\n**A gap is loss, and nothing is forwarded about it.** The judge, on an ingress `gap`, reconciles both ways against the inventory: an open exchange without a lease is granted afresh, and a leased exchange absent from the inventory is recorded `:lapsed` with cause `:policy`, so nothing is held unleased and nothing closed stays leased (inventory reconciliation; `dao.lease.md`'s reclaim-and-regrant is reserved for a lost ledger). The router forwards nothing about a gap: a request or terminal event lost in the evicted window is unrouted, and a forward whose `append!` answers anything but `ok` is the same loss; an unrouted request's exchange lapses by `:silence`. Drivers learn that an exchange is gone from the lease medium they already read for their grants: a `:lapsed` record for one of their exchanges drops it (`dao.lease.md`, Carriage). A driver whose cursor on any medium it reads answers `gap` forgets every subscription it holds and renews none; they lapse by `:silence` and close, and Datastar's reconnect subscribes again. Session media are declared at assembly like any deposit destination, evict-oldest with a capacity, and sized against the driver's lag.\n\n**Keep-alive is the responder's.** On its tick it may append an `:http/chunk` carrying an SSE comment line. That chunk is bytes for the wire, so the host discovers a dead peer by writing; the renewal is evidence for the judge. Neither stands in for the other. A host whose send buffers (http-kit) may not report a peer that disconnects while an exchange is idle; the exchange lapses by `:silence` and is closed by reclaim, with latency bounded by the lease duration plus judge cadence. A peer's close or a failed write is deposited as `:http/closed`; the responder reads it as data and drops the exchange, and the judge ends the exchange lease with cause `:policy`.\n\n**The lineage is leased** (owner ruling 2026-10-07; the holder choice below is the architect's). A lineage nobody returns to is reclaimed through `dao.lease.md`, not by a timer. The peer holding the parked record is the grantor. When a lineage begins, its judge grants unsolicited on the lease medium: subject and holder `{:dao.star/lineage S}`, duration the composition's idle allowance, `:dao.lease/max` optional. Attribution is by envelope key: the session-facts medium is written only by session drivers, each stamping `:dao.star/lineage` on its facts, renewals and release, and the judge's resolver reads that key; until ShiBi, any writer of that medium can renew, the same exposure the exchange lease has. The driver observes its grant before consuming or projecting, renews at under half the duration from its own control flow, on each consumed input and on its tick while it holds an open subscription, and **stops at its bound**: past it, it consumes nothing and projects nothing, and an input arriving then is handed to the root program as begin. So a connected session is retained for as long as it stays connected, by design, bounded only by the cap; a disconnected one lapses after the idle allowance. The lease ends with the lineage: released by the driver on halt, lapsed by the judge on silence. Reclaim retires the runnable resource, the current parked record; the `:lapsed` record on the lease medium is the judge's own and is what ends the lineage for the router. History, successor facts and lease records are kept (`dao.lease.md`: a reclaim frees the resource, never the record). The subject is distinct from Mode 2's custody subject `{:yin.k/occurrence O}` on the arbitration medium. Leases are process-scoped until the composition settles the three durable duties `dao.lease.md` names.\n\nSizing: duration exceeds twice the renewal interval; the keep-alive interval stays under the shortest proxy or NAT idle timeout on the path; the ingress window exceeds router and judge lag and each session medium's window exceeds its driver's; a boundary restart loses sockets and ledger together, so its exchange inventory to reclaim is empty. On hosts whose sends buffer, the exchange lease's duration is also the dead-peer detection bound. A false lapse is possible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\n\n---\n\n## Mode 2: The Fat Client (Mobile Continuations via UCF)\n\nStatus: proposed. Everything in this section waits on UCF version 1, fenced custody with a `dao.space` transactor as arbitration authority (`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` §7.7, 7.7.8). Version 0 is fork-only and cannot offer the exclusivity below.\n\nWhile `dao.star` champions the dumb terminal, it is not locked into it. Because `yin.vm` continuations are pure data, they can cross the network boundary to act as SPA-style fat clients for low-latency local interaction.\n\n### Flow\n\n1. **Exporting**: A `yin.vm` continuation on the server parks and enters the exporting state (UCF §7.7.4): its wait set is detached, so no poll or local resume can perform an effect while the value is in other hands. The lift produces a UCF handoff body, canonical and content-addressed, publishes it (*Handoff carrier*), and the driver records the offer on the arbitration medium.\n2. **Handoff**: The SSE patch carries the notice, not the body. A browser-side adapter deposits the notice on a browser-local stream that the browser's `yin.vm` reads; Datastar hands nothing to anyone.\n3. **Custody**: The browser `yin.vm` fetches the body by address over its WebSocket channel, proposes on the arbitration medium, and resumes only on observing its grant. Custody is exclusive because the lease is: the state moves to the browser rather than being shared with it, and the server's copy stays fenced.\n4. **Transparent Remote Access**: A continuation that needs a server-held stream uses the portable descriptors it carried and `attach!`es a reflection over WebSockets. Descriptors carry no authorization: the served entries are gated and leased for the migrated continuation (`dao.stream.remote.md`, sections 6 and 7). On channel loss a reflection answers `channel-gone` and reattachment is the continuation's own step on the same descriptor; a reclaimed entry answers `not-found` and is gone, not disconnected.\n5. **Migration Back**: The continuation re-serializes itself as a successor and offers it to the server, which admits it under *Admitting a continuation back*.\n\n### Handoff carrier\n\n**Architect ruling, not yet confirmed by the owner (2026-10-07).** The SSE patch carries a notice and the body travels by address over the browser's WebSocket channel. The notice is the body's content address, the arbitration descriptor, and a content coordinate `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :dao.jing/requests <remote descriptor> :dao.jing/answers <remote descriptor>}` (`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`, section 1).\n\nThe path: before the notice is appended, the lift publishes the body to the server's content store and appends the address to the session-facts medium as the lineage's export. The `requests` and `answers` entries are a pair served per lineage, so answers are not readable by other lineages, and gated with the lineage token as the pre-ShiBi credential; the gate's `verify` admits a `get` only for an address the lineage's own export facts name. The browser's fetch is the stepped content client, clock-free; it verifies the body by re-hashing (UCF §7.8, lower step 2). Three absences are distinct and all arrive as data: missing content is the service's `:jing/found? false`, a missing service is the remote `not-found` error, and a disallowed address is `:dao.stream/refused` at the gate. In each the browser holds nothing, and the exporter, a candidate like any other, proposes for itself under its own policy (UCF §7.7.4), so an unfetched offer strands nothing. Confidentiality is the gate's and the pair's: an address is a name, not an authorization.\n\nReasons: the body is canonical CBOR and SSE is text, so inlining means a second encoding; the egress medium is shared, and a body on it discloses the store slice to every observer, where a notice discloses a name; large values travel as content addresses (`dao.stream.md`, Granularity); and the browser `yin.vm` needs the channel anyway for custody and remote access, so the carrier adds no path.\n\nRejected: the body base64-encoded inside a `datastar-patch-signals` or element attribute (double encoding, disclosure on egress, Datastar's payload size); a second HTTP GET by address (a second boundary path for what the channel already carries).\n\n### Admitting a continuation back\n\nA continuation returning from the browser is admitted as untrusted code and state. It ran where nothing can attest to it, so it is treated as the controlled mode of `dao.space.security.md` treats any pushed interpreter.\n\n1. **Custody first** (UCF §7.7, unchanged). The successor must name, in `:yin.k/origin`, the lease the ledger holds live for that holder, and arrive on the holder's own medium. Otherwise `:yin.k/not-holder`.\n2. **Validate as hostile bytes** (UCF §7.8, lower steps 1 to 4), under a size bound and a step budget the composition declares.\n3. **Rebind authority; never read it from the value.** Lower into an isolated store. Primitives and FFI ops resolve from the receiver's registry, restricted to the set the composition delegated to this lineage at export. A stream marker attaches only if its identity is in that delegated set, or its channel is the returning peer's own. Anything else is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it.\n4. **No check survives the trip.** A decision the program made before export may have been skipped by a forged frame. Whatever must hold after return is enforced by a gate on a stream, or stays home in a continuation that never left and answers the migrated one over streams.\n\nThe test: a returned continuation can do nothing its principal could not do directly through the gated streams it was given.\n\n**Export is disclosure.** The lifted store slice and values are readable by the browser and by every peer the content gate admits. The composition inspects the computed closure before publishing.\n\n**Until effect handlers honor a delegated set** (`dao.space.security.md`, Honest Limits), migration back is admitted from allow-listed peers only (owner ruling 2026-10-07). ShiBi later plugs in at the same gates.\n\n---\n\n## Design Invariants Maintained\n\n- **No Shared Mutable State**: The UI is a pure projection (Mode 1), or custody of the continuation is held under an exclusive lease (Mode 2). State is never shared across the boundary; it moves, and the mover is fenced.\n- **Multiplexed Correlation**: Connections are not mapped 1:1 to streams. The HTTP boundary observes a shared egress stream and routes by exchange id. The cost is shared: an egress `gap` closes every open exchange, and a composition under that pressure shards the egress.\n- **Host Isolation**: The HTTP boundary is a dumb pipe that knows nothing of Datastar or `yin.vm` syntax. Datastar is a dumb renderer that knows nothing of backend state. Under Mode 2 a `yin.vm` runs beside it, not inside it.\n- **Data > Macros**: Compile-time slicing (Electric Clojure) is rejected. Network boundaries are crossed dynamically at runtime via reified data on explicit streams.\n- **No callbacks, no implicit control flow**: every arrival is a deposit, every departure is an effect, and every wait is a `blocked` answer at a cursor.\n- **No hidden state**: a driver's open subscriptions and the router's token index are interpreter state derived from media each reads with its own cursor, rebuilt from those media or honestly lost on a gap.\n\n## Deferred\n\n- **Multi-shot forks** (Arc's back button resuming a sibling branch): branch identity and subscription routing with a non-unique current are unspecified.\n- **Local projection** in Mode 2: the browser `yin.vm` appends the same Datastar-format effects to a browser-local egress stream read by a browser-side boundary adapter; same vocabulary, different boundary.\n- **Compaction of the session-facts medium**: waits on `dao.stream.md` OD-4.\n- **The content service as rules**: `dao.jing.content` is a plan section, not a spec; the notice above depends on it.\n- **An HTTP channel for peers without WebSocket**: `dao.stream.http.md`, Deferred.\n\n## Completion Criteria\n\n1. **Thin Client**: A `yin.vm` agent receives a Datastar input through the HTTP boundary's ingress stream, answers with `:http/head`, SSE-formatted `:http/chunk`s and `:http/end` on the egress stream, parks, and resumes on the next input for its current token. Also: a request with no token begins a lineage; a subscribe consumes nothing and a re-subscribe after several inputs receives the current projection; a stale token is re-projected; a closed socket is observed as `:http/closed`; a silent exchange lapses and closes; a lineage nobody returns to lapses, its parked record is retired and its facts remain; a request naming a just-published token is never answered as begin; after an ingress gap reconciliation leaves no exchange unleased and no closed exchange leased, and a driver stops renewing an exchange once it observes the lapse or its own gap; a renewal in flight may still count while the lease is live, and one observed after retirement does not revive it.\n2. **Fat Client Handoff**, conditional on UCF version 1 landing: a `yin.vm` agent exports under exclusive custody, the SSE patch carries the notice, a CLJS `yin.vm` in the browser fetches the body through the lineage's gated content pair, verifies it, obtains the grant, resumes, and reaches a server-held stream through a gated, leased reflection. Migration back from an allow-listed peer is admitted under *Admitting a continuation back*. The carrier shape is an architect ruling pending the owner's confirmation.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792832568086-stream-engineer-dao-star-m1-phase-a.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792833214850-stream-engineer-dao-star-m1-phase-b.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792832568086-architect-dao-star-m1-phase-b-scope.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792840534057-architect-dao-star-m1-client-close.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792832568086-stream-engineer-dao-star-m1-phase-a.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792833214850-stream-engineer-dao-star-m1-phase-b.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792832568086-architect-dao-star-m1-phase-b-scope.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792840534057-architect-dao-star-m1-client-close.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-24 (clock not read; no precise timestamp available)\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-24, +07 (clock not read)\n\nPhase A is implemented and its tests pass. I couldn't write the findings report or run `bb test:sub:clj dao.stream`, because both were denied by permissions.\n\n**Files** (all in the `dao-star-m1` worktree, nothing staged or committed):\n- `src/cljc/dao/stream/http.cljc`: the boundary core.\n- `src/clj/dao/stream/http/jvm.clj`: the http-kit listener.\n- `test/dao/stream/http_test.clj`: 11 tests.\n\n**Checks:**\n- `clojure -M:test -n dao.stream.http-test`: 11 tests, 86 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors.\n- `clojure -M:test -n dao.stream.http-test -r 'dao\\.stream\\..*-test'` ran 504 tests with 3874 assertions and 0 failures. I used it as a stand-in for the `dao.stream` lane. Two ws-project tests were skipped because the Dart peer binary isn't built.\n- `clj -M:kondo --lint` on the three files: 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- `bb test:sub:clj dao.stream` was not run: `bb` isn't on PATH, and calling the mise shim was refused.\n- The findings report was not written to the main tree's `collab/`, because the write was denied. The contents are summarised below.\n- I wrote the tests and the code in one pass and ran the tests afterwards, so this wasn't strict test-first.\n\n**Decisions to confirm:**\n- **Lifetime key:** The spec's \"lifetime longer than the boundary\" has no key in the ws admission check. I invented `:lifetime :outlives-boundary` and require it.\n- **Incarnation:** `:http/incarnation` comes from config, or defaults to a fresh uuid string.\n- **Stricter than the spec:** I treat a head with a non-integer status, a chunk body that isn't a string or bytes, and an `:http/end` before the head as out of order and close the exchange. The spec names only chunk-before-head and a second head.\n- **Step behaviour:** `egress-step` drains until the egress medium is empty. A writer that keeps appending could keep it running.\n\n**Not covered:**\n- The 400 path (an exception inside `accept!`) is untested.\n- Egress `transport-error` shares the branch with `end`, but only `end` is tested.\n- A peer disconnecting mid-response is covered only with scripted sockets. I rely on http-kit's `send!` returning false on a closed channel.\n- The race where an exchange closes while its request event is still being deposited has no test.\n- I did not run the host-boundary gate (`gate_test`). The cljs and cljd listeners are out of scope.\n- The spec's Deferred list is left unimplemented, as intended.\n\nI can write the report once you grant write access to that `collab/` path.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-24 (clock not read; no precise timestamp available)\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-24, +07 (clock not read)\n\nPhase B is implemented and its tests pass. The findings write to `collab/` was denied, so the full report is below for you to promote.\n\n## Changed files\nAll in the `dao-star-m1` worktree, nothing staged or committed.\n- `src/cljc/dao/star/`:\n  - `facts.cljc`: fact constructors, `valid-fact?`, `index-step`, `resolve-token`.\n  - `signals.cljc`: `decode`, using `clojure.data.json` on the JVM.\n  - `lease.cljc`: the optional fifth namespace, holding the stream `drain` helper, the holder agent, and the exchange-lease and lineage-lease grantor wrappers.\n  - `router.cljc`: pure `classify` and a stepped router.\n  - `driver.cljc`: per-session `dao.await` VM, begin handler, and the lease logic.\n- `test/dao/star/`: `facts`, `signals`, `router`, `driver` (also the public harness), `loopback` and `peer_close` tests.\n\n## Outcomes\n- **Phase B tests:** `clojure -M:test -n dao.star.facts-test -n dao.star.signals-test -n dao.star.router-test -n dao.star.driver-test -n dao.star.loopback-test -n dao.star.peer-close-test`: 34 tests, 590 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors. The `^:slow` stress test is included in that count.\n- **Stream lane stand-in:** the same run plus `dao.stream.http-test` and `-r 'dao\\.stream\\..*-test'`: 538 tests, 4464 assertions, 0 failures.\n- **`bb test:sub:clj dao.stream`:** not run, since `bb` was not permitted this session.\n- **kondo:** clean (0 errors, 0 warnings) on all `src/cljc/dao/star`, `test/dao/star` and the Phase A files.\n- **Process:** I wrote the code before the driver tests, not strictly test-first. `facts` and `signals` were verified first, then `router`, then the code and tests for `driver` and `loopback` together.\n\n## Completion Criteria item 1 coverage\n- **Begin without a token:** `request-with-no-token-begins-a-lineage`.\n- **Agent answers, parks, resumes:** `agent-answers-parks-and-resumes-on-the-next-input` in memory, and `agent-receives-answers-and-resumes-over-the-real-boundary` on loopback. The VM is a real `dao.await` continuation, and the driver does all the I/O.\n- **Subscribe consumes nothing:** `subscribe-consumes-nothing-and-resubscribe-gets-the-current-projection`.\n- **Stale token re-projected:** `stale-token-is-reprojected-and_runs-nothing`.\n- **Fact before UI:** `no-ui-with-the-successor-id-before-its-fact-is-accepted`. It uses a gated facts medium, and the driver retries the pending fact until it answers `ok`.\n- **Closed socket:** `a-closed-socket-is-observed-as-http-closed` on loopback, plus `a-peer-close-is-read-as-data-and-drops-the-subscription` in memory. See concern 1 below.\n- **Silent exchange lapses:** `silent-exchange-lapses-and-closes`, driven by hand-appended ticks.\n- **Unreturned lineage lapses:** `unreturned-lineage-lapses-record-retired-facts-remain`.\n- **Bound handling:** `a-driver-at-its-bound-consumes-nothing-and-begins-instead`.\n- **Halt:** `the-program-halting-ends-the-lineage-and-releases`.\n- **Just-published token:** `just-published-token-is-never-begin` (25 chained posts) and a `^:slow` stress variant (400 chained posts).\n- **Ingress gap:** `after-an-ingress-gap-no-exchange-unleased-and-no-closed-exchange-leased`.\n- **Driver stops renewing:** `driver-stops-renewing-after-observing-the-lapse` and `driver-stops-renewing-after-its-own-gap`.\n- **Renewal in flight:** `renewal-in-flight-counts-while-live-and-does-not-revive-a-retired-lease`.\n- **Router:** its order, co-location refusal (`:refused :co-located`), gap and failed-forward behaviour are in `router_test.clj`.\n\n## Concerns and deviations\n1. **http-kit never reports a client-initiated close** of an idle streaming exchange. A minimal repro with no dao code showed no `on-close` after FIN or RST, and 40 further writes all returned true. So Phase A's `peer-closed!` is unreachable by a real client disconnect through the current `jvm.clj`.\n   - The loopback closed-socket test closes from the boundary side with `close-exchange` instead, and asserts that `:http/closed` lands on ingress and the driver drops the subscription.\n   - The in-memory test covers a peer close deposited by a fake boundary.\n   - `close-exchange` ends the chunked response and http-kit keeps the connection alive, so the client sees the chunk terminator, not TCP EOF.\n   - Fixing the real client-close detection needs an owner or Architect decision, since `jvm.clj` is a Phase A file I may not edit.\n2. **Datastar wording:** no `src/` code mentions Datastar or UCF. The `datastar` query-parameter name appears as a literal in `signals.cljc`, because the spec dictates it.\n3. **Programs avoid `loop`/`recur`:** yang.clojure has neither, so the test programs recurse through `(def lp (fn ...))`. A literal map containing a variable is also treated as a constant, so programs build models with `assoc`.\n4. **Reclaim success:** for the exchange lease it reports success when the exchange is no longer open afterwards, not the raw boolean from `close-exchange`. Otherwise an exchange the peer already closed would stall the judge.\n5. **Policy:** the exchange lease's policy ends a lease with cause `:policy` whenever its exchange is absent from the inventory. That covers both peer close and gap reconciliation.\n6. **Noise handling:** routed messages are not validated beyond their `:kind`. Facts and lease records on the facts medium are judged by `valid-fact?` and `index-step`.\n7. **Not covered:**\n   - Roots lost in a session-facts gap get no lineage grant; the spec is silent on this.\n   - Requests to paths other than `/`, `/s/…` and `/k/…` are unrouted and lapse by silence.\n   - cljs and cljd were not run, and the host-boundary gate test was not run.\n   - Mode 2 and Datastar-specific behaviour are out of scope.\n8. **Router co-location check:** it uses a `ReflectionHandle` instance check on the JVM and otherwise relies on the declared `:co-located? true`.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-24 09:06:16 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-24 16:06:16 +0700\n\nI edited no files. The sources I read are `dao.star.md`, `dao.stream.http.md`, `dao.lease.md`, `lease.cljc` (the `make-judge`, `make-holder`, `initial-holder`, `observe-grant` and `renewal-interval` entry points), `ringbuffer.cljc`, `waitset.cljc`, `engine.cljc` (`check-wait-set`), `await.cljc` and `waitset/driver.cljs`. I did not read the stream.md, UCF or streams-all-the-way-down documents. I did not read `dao.stream.md` (envelopes, deposit admission, OD-4). The OD-4 point in ruling 3 comes only from `dao.star.md`'s own text. I also did not read the continuation or UCF documents or the `ucf/rows` source. The rulings below rest on `await.cljc` and `engine.cljc`, which I did read.\n\nThe worktree has no HTTP boundary code yet, so Phase B's contract is the `dao.stream.http.md` text: `open-exchanges`, `close-exchange`, `egress-step` and the ingress/egress media.\n\n## 1. Namespace layout\n\n**Ruling:** four small namespaces under `src/cljc/dao/star/`, with no `dao.star` umbrella that holds logic.\n\n| Namespace | Owns |\n|---|---|\n| `dao.star.facts` | The three-fact grammar as pure functions: `root`, `consumption`, `ended`, `valid-fact?`, and `index-step` (fold one fact or lapse into the occurrence → lineage → current index). |\n| `dao.star.signals` | Ruling 7. |\n| `dao.star.router` | Ruling 4: a pure `classify` plus a stepped `router-step`. |\n| `dao.star.driver` | Rulings 2, 5 and 6: per-session parked state, the step, lease holder state and subscription state. |\n\nA small `dao.star.lease` for the two judge/holder compositions is optional. It is worth it only if the exchange-judge wiring would otherwise bloat `dao.star.driver`. I'd start inside `dao.star.driver` and split if the file passes about 400 lines.\n\n**Reasons:**\n- This matches the repo's granularity: one concern per namespace, as in `dao.stream.ringbuffer`, `.waitset` and `.ws-project`.\n- `facts` and `signals` are pure and portable `.cljc`. They stay testable without streams and reusable when Mode 2 changes the driver.\n- The Datastar vocabulary is not in any of them (see ruling 6).\n\n**Rejected:**\n- A single `dao.star` namespace. It would mix pure grammar with stepped interpreters.\n- A `dao.star.facts` that owns the stream. The facts medium belongs to the composition (ruling 3).\n\n## 2. The parked continuation\n\n**Ruling:** option (a). A real `yin.vm` CESK continuation parked on a stream wait is buildable on the JVM today and belongs in Phase B. It needs no UCF v1, because Mode 1 never migrates the continuation.\n\n**The integration point:** `dao.await/run` and `dao.await/resume` (`src/cljc/dao/await.cljc`).\n- `run` builds a fresh ast-walker VM, attaches env stream handles via `engine/attach-resource`, evaluates, and returns `{:vm :blocked? :value :proc}`.\n- `resume` calls `vm/eval vm nil`. The engine's `check-wait-set` then runs `dao.stream.waitset/check` over the parked `:next` entries and re-queues the woken ones.\n- The driver's \"poll\" is therefore `await/resume`, which is the `waitset/check` sweep. The cljs `waitset/driver.cljs` is only a timer shape, and the JVM driver is a plain loop.\n\n**What the driver holds and steps, per session:**\n- The `:dao.await/result` map, which is the parked record.\n- Two ring-buffer handles. The input medium carries routed inputs `{:exchange x :signals {...}}`. The view medium carries the model the program emits.\n- The driver's cursors, the lease holder state and the open-subscription set.\n\n**One step, in this order:**\n1. The driver reads its routed-input cursor.\n2. On a `value` for a current-token input, it appends the input to the VM's input medium.\n3. It calls `await/resume` on the parked result.\n4. It drains the VM's view medium, which holds the new model.\n5. It mints the successor occurrence O2.\n6. It appends the consumption fact with `:dao.stream/ok`.\n7. Only after that `ok` does it project, and it appends `:http/head`, one `:http/chunk` per SSE event and `:http/end` (or only a chunk, for a subscription exchange) to egress.\n\n**Why the program emits a model, not egress effects:** the spec requires the fact to be accepted before any UI carrying O2 is projected. If the program appended to egress itself, it would bypass that ordering. The view medium is the program's only output.\n\n**The honest reading of \"A yin.vm agent\":**\n- A real VM continuation is parked and then resumed by the driver observing a stream value. The mechanism is the existing `check-wait-set` path.\n- The driver, not the VM program, performs the HTTP effects and the fact appends. This keeps ordering and the lease in one place.\n- This reading should be stated in the Phase B brief and in the criterion's test. The driver is the only part that does I/O, and the program is pure sequential logic over `<!` and `>!`.\n\n**Rejected:**\n- (b), a bespoke step function over parked state. It would not exercise the CESK parking surface at all, and the criterion names a `yin.vm` agent.\n- The program appending to egress directly. It breaks fact-before-UI ordering.\n- Waiting for UCF v1. Mode 1 continuations are one-shot and never move.\n- Extracting local variables from the CESK frames for projection. That is not a stable surface; the emitted model is.\n\n**Caveat for the brief:** the parked VM holds a host-local stream handle for its input. That is fine in one process. Mode 2 is where it stops being portable.\n\n## 3. Session-facts medium\n\n**Ruling:** a `dao.stream.ringbuffer` created by the composition, evict-oldest, with a capacity sized above the router's and the lineage judge's lag. The constructor call is `ringbuffer/create!` with `:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ringbuffer` and `:dao.stream.ringbuffer/capacity n`.\n\n**Enforcement of the grammar** belongs to the writer, not the medium:\n- The driver builds facts only through `dao.star.facts/root|consumption|ended`.\n- The router and the lineage-lease resolver treat a read fact that fails `valid-fact?` as noise: they ignore it and never throw on it.\n\n**Rejected:**\n- A journal, which is durable. Compaction waits on OD-4 and is deferred in the spec.\n- A guard middleware on the medium that rejects malformed facts. The medium stays payload-agnostic, per the \"dao.jing is syntax, agents are semantics\" stance.\n\n## 4. Router\n\n**Ruling:** a stepped `router-step`, threading `{ingress-cursor facts-cursor lease-cursor index}`.\n\n**One pass, in the spec's order:**\n1. Read ingress with its own cursor until `blocked`, collecting events, including a `gap` marker if one appears.\n2. Drain the session-facts cursor to `blocked`, folding each fact into the index via `facts/index-step`.\n3. Drain the lineage-lease cursor to `blocked`, folding `:lapsed` records into the index. A lapsed lineage is ended for routing.\n4. Classify what step 1 read.\n\n**Classification (`dao.star.router/classify`, pure, over index plus request):**\n- `/` is begin.\n- `/s/<t>` is subscribe if the token resolves to a live lineage, else begin.\n- `/k/<t>` is input if `t` is the lineage's current occurrence, stale if `t` is consumed, and begin if the lineage is ended, lapsed or unknown.\n\n**Unknown is judged only after both drains.**\n\n**Co-location constraint:** an assembly-time assertion in the router constructor. The facts and lease handles must be direct, non-reflection handles, tested with `(not (satisfies? ... reflection))` or an explicit `:co-located? true` declaration at assembly. It throws ex-info with `:refused`, as the lease constructors do.\n\n**Forwarding:**\n- A request goes to the session medium, or to the begin handler's medium for `begin`.\n- A terminal event (`:http/ended` or `:http/closed`) goes to whichever session holds that exchange. The router keeps `exchange → session` as interpreter state, built only from what it routed.\n- On an ingress `gap` it forwards nothing. The lost window is unrouted, and those exchanges lapse by `:silence`.\n- A forward whose `append!` is not `ok` is the same loss and is dropped silently.\n- A terminal event for an unrouted exchange is ignored.\n\n**Rejected:** a router that keeps a token table independent of the facts. The spec forbids it.\n\n## 5. Lease compositions\n\n**Exchange lease (inside the boundary peer):**\n- **Judge:** `lease/make-judge` with `:reclaim` = `(fn [subject] (http/close-exchange (:http/exchange subject)))`, which must be idempotent and report success.\n- **Writer:** the lease medium.\n- **Media:** the ingress cursor, so the judge sees `:http/request`.\n- **Ticks:** a dedicated tick stream for the judge.\n- **Required config:** an explicit `:tolerance` (`{:ms 0}` for the zero), a `:cadence` and a `:resolver`.\n- **Inventory:** `open-exchanges` feeds gap reconciliation, as `dao.star.md`, Liveness, specifies.\n- **Grants:** a stepped wrapper authors an unsolicited grant per `:http/request` through `lease/author-grant`. Subject is `{:http/exchange x}`, holder is the egress identity, and the duration is something like `{:s 30}`.\n- **Reclaim:** closing the exchange makes the adapter deposit `:http/closed`, and then the judge records `:lapsed`.\n\n**Exchange holder (driver side):** `lease/make-holder` with `:self` = the egress identity and `:renewal-interval` from `lease/renewal-interval` (strictly under half the duration). It observes its grant, renews through `due-to-renew?` and `observe-renewal`, and stops on a `:lapsed` record. A one-shot responder renews nothing.\n\n**Lineage lease:**\n- **Grantor:** the peer holding the parked records, so the lineage judge is the driver's own judge, on the same peer as the router and the drivers.\n- **Judge:** `lease/make-judge` over the session-facts and lease media. `:resolver-bindings` must cover envelope-key attribution (`:dao.star/lineage`). The reclaim procedure drops the parked record: the `await` result and the session's media handles.\n- **Grant:** unsolicited at lineage begin, subject and holder `{:dao.star/lineage S}`, duration being the idle allowance.\n- **Holder per lineage:** `make-holder` with `:subject {:dao.star/lineage S}`. It threads this state:\n  - `observe-grant` before the first consume or project;\n  - `due-to-renew?` on each consumed input and on a tick while a subscription is open;\n  - `at-bound?` to stop at the cap, after which it consumes and projects nothing, and an arriving input is handed to the root program as begin;\n  - `release` on halt.\n- **Where the state threads:** the driver's per-session map holds the holder state. One holder state per lease, because the holder discipline keeps one state per lease, and a lineage is one lease.\n\n**Rejected:** a shared holder for several lineages.\n\n## 6. The projection seam\n\n**Ruling:** `(project-fn model) → string`, a pure function the composition supplies to the driver. The returned string is the complete SSE wire text, such as `event: datastar-patch-elements\\ndata: …\\n\\n`.\n\n**Where Datastar lives:** inside `project-fn` and any helper the composition builds. `dao.star` has no `datastar` namespace. It ships one reference projection for the tests, in a test or example namespace, not in `src/`.\n\n**What the model contains:**\n- It is the program's emitted model.\n- The occurrence id must reach the projection, since the spec says every projected UI carries the id of the occurrence that rendered it, so the seam is `(project-fn occurrence-id model)`.\n- The root begin page, which is a full HTML document, is a separate supplied `begin-fn`. It is not an SSE patch.\n\n**Re-projection (subscribe and stale):** the driver keeps the latest `{:occurrence O :model m}` as part of its parked record and calls the same `project-fn` on it without calling `resume`. This is why the model is stored: re-projection never touches the continuation.\n\n**Rejected:** a `project-fn` that reads the VM state. It could not be used for stale and subscribe without stepping.\n\n## 7. Signals decode\n\n**Ruling:** `dao.star.signals/decode` is a pure function from an `:http/request` event to a signals map.\n- GET and DELETE read the `datastar` query parameter, URL-decoded and JSON-parsed.\n- Other methods parse the JSON body.\n- A missing, malformed or non-map input gives `{}`. Decode never throws, because signals are the client's unverified claim.\n\n**Where it runs:** in the driver. The router forwards the raw request and decodes nothing, which keeps the ingress tuple unchanged and the signals non-persistent.\n\n**JSON:** use the parser the repo already uses on the JVM, and check which one the boundary needs before the brief names it. I did not check.\n\n## 8. Test plan\n\nAll tests run on the JVM against the real boundary from Phase A. Items marked **L** need real loopback sockets, and the rest run on in-memory ring buffers and a fake boundary that implements `open-exchanges`, `close-exchange` and the egress/ingress media.\n\n| Clause | Test | Medium |\n|---|---|---|\n| Agent receives input, answers head/chunks/end, parks, resumes on next input | A VM program that loops `<!`/`>!`. Post twice and assert the second response reflects the first. | **L** for one end-to-end test; the rest in-memory |\n| Request with no token begins a lineage | A `/` request produces a root fact and a complete HTML document. | in-memory |\n| Subscribe consumes nothing; a re-subscribe after several inputs gets the current projection | Three inputs, a subscribe, and a check that no consumption fact was added. | in-memory |\n| Stale token is re-projected | Post the same token twice. The second runs nothing and gets the current projection plus `:http/end`. | in-memory |\n| Closed socket observed as `:http/closed` | Client disconnects mid-subscription. | **L** |\n| Silent exchange lapses and closes | Ticks past the duration, then assert `close-exchange` and `:lapsed`. | in-memory (fake clock via tick stream) |\n| Unreturned lineage lapses, record retired, facts remain | Tick past the idle allowance, assert the parked record is gone and the facts are still readable. | in-memory |\n| Request naming a just-published token is never answered as begin | A tight loop of post, read UI id, post immediately. | in-memory, with a stress variant |\n| After an ingress gap, no exchange unleased and no closed exchange leased | Overflow a small ingress ring, then reconcile. | in-memory |\n| Driver stops renewing after observing the lapse or its own gap | Assert no renewal appended after each. | in-memory |\n| Renewal in flight counts while live; one observed after retirement does not revive | Interleave renewal and `:lapsed` on the lease medium. | in-memory |\n\n**`^:slow` candidates:** the stress variant of the just-published-token test, and the loopback test if it needs real time. Everything else belongs in the quick tier.\n\n**Coverage:** this plan covers all ten clauses fully on the JVM if the Phase A boundary is accurate. Two things it defers:\n- Cljs and cljd hosts are untested.\n- A real long-lived SSE subscription with a browser is not tested. A raw socket client stands in for Datastar.\n\n## 9. File ownership and risks\n\n**Ownership for the Phase B implementer:**\n- Creates `src/cljc/dao/star/{facts,signals,router,driver}.cljc` and the matching tests under `test/dao/star/`.\n- Does not touch `dao.lease`, `dao.stream.*`, `dao.await`, `yin.vm.*` or the Phase A boundary files. Changes there go back to the Architect.\n- Does not touch the worktree `dao-star-m1`'s Phase A files while that delegate is running. Phase B should start only after Phase A lands.\n\n**Risks, in order of likelihood:**\n1. **Phase A API drift.** I could not read the real `open-exchanges` and `close-exchange` signatures. The brief must pin them down once Phase A lands.\n2. **`await/resume` re-evaluates the whole VM.** Each call runs the sweep over every parked entry in that VM. This is fine per session, but the brief should say one VM per session, not one VM for all sessions.\n3. **Fact-before-UI ordering.** An implementer may project before the fact's `ok`. The brief must say the driver blocks on the append outcome and test it.\n4. **Router classification races.** Reading ingress after the drains instead of before breaks the proof. The brief should pin the order and the test that checks it.\n5. **Lease sizing.** Duration must exceed twice the renewal interval plus the tick period. Use `lease/renewal-interval` with a tick period rather than hand-picked numbers.\n6. **Reclaim idempotence.** `close-exchange` must be idempotent and must report success. A reclaim that throws on an already-closed exchange stalls the judge.\n7. **Resolver attribution.** The lineage lease's resolver reads the `:dao.star/lineage` envelope key. A fact's own claim about its author must never be consulted, which `observe-grant` already enforces.\n8. **Scope creep into Mode 2.** The brief should state that no UCF, custody or export code is in Phase B.\n\n**Open for the owner:** the \"A yin.vm agent\" reading in ruling 2 (driver does I/O, program emits a model). I recommend it.\n\n**Test coverage summary:** I cover all ten semicolon-separated scenarios of Completion Criteria item 1 on the JVM. Criterion item 2 (Fat Client) is deferred and conditional on UCF v1.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-24 11:16:31 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-24 18:16:31 +0700\n\nI edited nothing. I read `jvm.clj` (lines 23–63) and the context in your prompt. I did not re-run the http-kit repro.\n\n## Ruling: (a), with a small wording correction. `jvm.clj` does not change.\n\n### What \"a failed socket write closes the exchange\" means when the host cannot tell\n\nThe sentence in `dao.stream.http.md` is conditional: the boundary acts on a failure the host reports. The seam reports failure only by throwing or answering false. An http-kit `kit/send!` that buffers and answers true is not a failure report, so the boundary has nothing to act on.\n\nThis is the contract's irreducible corner, not a violation. The boundary cannot invent an outcome the host never gave it, and the document already says a performed write is not a delivery (\"the step appends no per-effect outcome\"). A write that succeeds into a buffer is a performed write, not a delivered one.\n\nWhat the sentence promises is detection when the host can detect. Where it cannot, `dao.star.md` already names the backstop: the judge's `:silence` lapse and the `close-exchange` reclaim.\n\n### Why (a)\n\n- **It changes no code and almost no text.** The designed liveness path covers the case, and the spec calls a one-shot wire write \"bytes for the wire\", not evidence.\n- **The lease is the detector the spec already built.** Keep-alive chunks and renewals were deliberately made two separate things, and \"neither stands in for the other.\" Under (a) the keep-alive chunk is still wire bytes, and the renewal is still the judge's evidence. A dead peer that http-kit hides simply stops being useful to anyone, so the responder's own control flow stops renewing for it and the lease lapses. In practice the responder drops an exchange whose `:http/closed` it reads, and an unread dead exchange lapses.\n- **Latency is bounded and sizeable by the composition.** It is the exchange lease duration plus the judge cadence. The Sizing paragraph already makes the duration the composition's choice, so the keep-alive interval and duration can be sized to the latency it wants.\n\n### Rejected alternatives\n\n- **(b) Block on the `kit/send!` promise per write.**\n  - A bounded wait puts a timeout, which is a clock, into `egress-step`. The boundary's design says the step \"takes no clock and holds no timer.\"\n  - Waiting on delivery also contradicts \"a performed write is not a delivery.\"\n  - I also can't confirm that dereferencing the promise would surface a closed peer. The repro showed buffered sends answering true 40 times, so a deref might still return true. You would be adding a stall with no evidence it detects anything.\n  - It would reopen a verified Phase A file for a speculative fix.\n- **(c) A third \"unknown\" answer from `:write!`.**\n  - Core already treats \"no failure reported\" as \"unknown,\" because it never learns delivery. A third value would make explicit what the behavior already is, at the cost of changing the seam contract in `http.cljc` and every host that implements it.\n  - It would add an outcome nothing consumes.\n  - It is the largest text change of the three, so it fails your \"least text\" criterion.\n\n### Does the criterion \"a closed socket is observed as `:http/closed`\" pass?\n\nIt passes for a closure the host does report, and it does not pass for an undetected client disconnect on http-kit. The criterion should be read as \"when the host reports a close or failed write, it is deposited as `:http/closed`.\" The honest test splits in two:\n\n1. **Host-reported close.** A seam-level test where the fake or real host triggers the `on-close` path, `peer-closed!`, and asserts exactly one `:http/closed` is deposited and the exchange is dropped by the driver. This covers the boundary's behavior. If the Phase B loopback can trigger an actual `on-close` another way (for example, the server closes, or a client closes mid-request before the response head), use that; the repro shows only the idle-streaming case is silent.\n2. **Silent peer.** The reclaim-path test the delegate already wrote: the exchange lapses by `:silence`, `close-exchange` runs, the adapter deposits `:http/closed`, and then the judge records `:lapsed`. This is the honest end-to-end test for a half-dead exchange on http-kit.\n\nThe owner should be told plainly that item 2 is the test for a real client disconnect on this host. It is not a real-disconnect test. The completion criteria text for the clause can stay, with the Honest Limit below.\n\n### What must change\n\n**`jvm.clj`:** nothing. The `:write!` at line 29 and the `:on-close` handler at lines 61–63 are as the contract intends.\n\n**Docs, minimal:** one Honest Limit entry, in `dao.stream.http.md` beside \"Observably gone\" or in a Limits section, and a matching sentence in `dao.star.md`'s Liveness: \"A host whose send buffers (http-kit) may not report a peer that disconnects while an exchange is idle; the exchange lapses by `:silence` and is closed by reclaim, with latency bounded by the lease duration plus judge cadence.\" Name http-kit as the instance, not as the rule, so another host (cljs or cljd) is not presumed to behave the same.\n\n**Optional, no code:** if the owner wants faster detection later, the right place is a host that exposes real socket state to `:write!` (Netty, or http-kit with a TCP-level probe). That is a host change under the existing seam, not a seam change.\n\n### Residual risk to record\n\n- A false negative window exists: between a client's disconnect and the lapse, the responder may keep projecting patches to a dead exchange. Buffered writes are bounded by http-kit's own buffering, not by dao.star. That is a resource cost, bounded by the lease duration.\n- If someone later sets a very long exchange duration for a quiet subscription, detection latency grows with it. Sizing guidance should say the duration is also the dead-peer detection bound on hosts like this.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/cljc/dao/stream/http.cljc src/clj/dao/stream/http/jvm.clj'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat src/cljc/dao/stream/http.cljc src/clj/dao/stream/http/jvm.clj'","aggregated_output":"(ns dao.stream.http\n  \"The DaoStream HTTP boundary (docs/design/dao.stream.http.md).\n\n   The boundary is an adapter (events in) and a host effect interpreter\n   (effects out) over two media the composition supplies: an ingress medium it\n   deposits into and an egress medium it reads.  It owns no stream, hands no\n   handle upward, and never reads a request body, query or response chunk.\n\n   This namespace knows no HTTP library.  A host listener owns its sockets and\n   supplies, per exchange, a small synchronous socket seam:\n\n     :head!  (fn [status headers])  write the response head\n     :write! (fn [chunk])           write bytes or a string (UTF-8) and flush\n     :end!   (fn [])                complete the response\n     :close! (fn [])                drop the connection, no response\n\n   A seam function reports failure by throwing or answering false.  Socket\n   callbacks enter only through `accept!` and `peer-closed!`; the step takes no\n   clock and holds no timer.\"\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\n            [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]\n            [dao.host.text :as text]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]))\n\n\n(defn admission?\n  \"The ingress deposit declaration: evict-oldest retention with a positive\n   declared capacity, value domain `:portable-values`, and a lifetime longer\n   than the boundary (`:lifetime :outlives-boundary`).  A reject-mode\n   destination, or one that cannot carry every envelope, is not admissible.\"\n  [x]\n  (and (map? x)\n       (= :evict-oldest (:retention x))\n       (integer? (:capacity x))\n       (pos? (:capacity x))\n       (= :portable-values (:value-domain x))\n       (= :outlives-boundary (:lifetime x))))\n\n\n(defn- target-with?\n  [target surface pred]\n  (and (map? target)\n       (pred (:dao.stream/handle target))\n       (contains? (:dao.stream/surface target) surface)))\n\n\n(defn- positive-int?\n  [x]\n  (and (integer? x) (pos? x)))\n\n\n(defn make-boundary\n  \"Construct the boundary.  Config:\n\n     :ingress            writer target {:dao.stream/handle :dao.stream/surface}\n     :ingress-admission  its declaration, checked by `admission?`\n     :egress             reader target the boundary reads effects from\n     :http/max-open      exchanges held open at once (positive integer)\n     :http/max-body-bytes  largest request body deposited (positive integer)\n     :http/incarnation   optional portable value distinguishing this\n                         boundary from any earlier one on the same egress\n                         medium; defaults to a fresh uuid string\n\n   Wiring a reject-mode destination, or a destination that is not a writer,\n   is an assembly defect and throws.  The egress cursor is minted at\n   `:dao.stream/newest` here, before any listener starts.\"\n  [{:keys [ingress ingress-admission egress]\n    max-open :http/max-open\n    max-body :http/max-body-bytes\n    :as config}]\n  (when-not (and (target-with? ingress :writer stream/writer?)\n                 (admission? ingress-admission)\n                 (target-with? egress :reader stream/reader?)\n                 (positive-int? max-open)\n                 (positive-int? max-body))\n    (throw (ex-info \"invalid DaoStream HTTP boundary composition\"\n                    {:config (dissoc config :ingress :egress)})))\n  (let [minted (stream/cursor (:dao.stream/handle egress) stream/anchor-newest)]\n    (when-not (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome minted))\n      (throw (ex-info \"cannot mint the egress cursor\" {:outcome minted})))\n    {:ingress (:dao.stream/handle ingress)\n     :egress (:dao.stream/handle egress)\n     :max-open max-open\n     :max-body-bytes max-body\n     :incarnation (or (:http/incarnation config) (str (random-uuid)))\n     :state (atom {:cursor (:dao.stream/cursor minted)\n                   :n 0\n                   :open {}\n                   :stopped? false\n                   :on-stop nil})}))\n\n\n;; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; Deposits and the seam\n;; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n(defn- deposit!\n  \"Deposit one event unjudged; true when the ingress medium admitted it.\"\n  [boundary event]\n  (= :dao.stream/ok\n     (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/append! (:ingress boundary) event))))\n\n\n(defn- seam-ok?\n  \"Run a socket seam call; false when it threw or answered false.\"\n  [f & args]\n  (try\n    (not (false? (apply f args)))\n    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ false)))\n\n\n(defn- drop-socket!\n  [entry]\n  (when-let [close! (:close! (:socket entry))]\n    (seam-ok? close!)))\n\n\n(defn- terminal-event\n  [id event]\n  {:http/exchange id :http/event event})\n\n\n(defn- finish!\n  \"Deposit the one terminal event of an already-claimed exchange.  An exchange\n   whose request event is still being deposited records the terminal on its\n   entry instead; `accept!` deposits it straight after the request.\"\n  [boundary id entry event]\n  (if (:requested? entry)\n    (deposit! boundary (terminal-event id event))\n    (let [pending? (volatile! false)]\n      ;; The entry was claimed (removed) by the caller; park the event on the\n      ;; side table for the accepting call to find.\n      (swap! (:state boundary)\n             (fn [s]\n               (if (contains? (:awaiting s) id)\n                 (do (vreset! pending? true)\n                     (assoc-in s [:awaiting id] event))\n                 s)))\n      (when-not @pending?\n        (deposit! boundary (terminal-event id event))))))\n\n\n(defn- claim!\n  \"Atomically remove exchange `id` from the open set; its entry, or nil when\n   this boundary does not hold it open.\"\n  [boundary id]\n  (let [claimed (volatile! nil)]\n    (swap! (:state boundary)\n           (fn [s]\n             (if-let [entry (get-in s [:open id])]\n               (do (vreset! claimed entry)\n                   (update s :open dissoc id))\n               s)))\n    @claimed))\n\n\n(defn- close-claimed!\n  \"Observably gone: drop the socket this boundary holds and deposit the\n   terminal `:http/closed`.\"\n  [boundary id entry]\n  (drop-socket! entry)\n  (finish! boundary id entry :http/closed))\n\n\n(defn close-exchange\n  \"The idempotent reclaim procedure for a `dao.lease.md` judge composed inside\n   this boundary: close the socket of exchange `id` and deposit its terminal\n   `:http/closed`.  Answers true when it closed an open exchange, false when\n   there was none.  Not for code written against the contract.\"\n  [boundary id]\n  (if-let [entry (claim! boundary id)]\n    (do (close-claimed! boundary id entry) true)\n    false))\n\n\n(defn open-exchanges\n  \"The ids of the exchanges currently held open, the inventory a\n   `dao.lease.md` judge needs after a restart or an ingress `gap`.\"\n  [boundary]\n  (set (keys (:open @(:state boundary)))))\n\n\n(defn- close-all!\n  [boundary]\n  (doseq [id (open-exchanges boundary)]\n    (close-exchange boundary id)))\n\n\n;; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; Events in\n;; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n(defn- none?\n  [x]\n  (or (nil? x)\n      (and (string? x) (empty? x))\n      (and (bytes/bytes? x) (zero? (bytes/length x)))))\n\n\n(defn- request-event\n  [id {:keys [method path query headers body]}]\n  (cond-> {:http/exchange id\n           :http/event :http/request\n           :http/method (keyword (str/lower-case (name method)))\n           :http/path path\n           :http/headers (into {} (map (fn [[k v]] [(str/lower-case (str k)) v])) headers)}\n    (not (none? query)) (assoc :http/query query)\n    (not (none? body)) (assoc :http/body body)))\n\n\n(defn- body-size\n  [body]\n  (cond (nil? body) 0\n        (string? body) (bytes/length (text/utf8-encode body))\n        :else (bytes/length body)))\n\n\n(defn accept!\n  \"Adapter entry: one valid, framed request arrived with its `socket` seam.\n   `request` is `{:method :path :query :headers :body}` as the host parsed it.\n   Answers `{:http/status :http/accepted :http/exchange id}`, or\n   `{:http/status :http/full}`, `:http/too-large` or `:http/stopped` when the\n   request must be answered below the transform (503, 413, 503) and was not\n   deposited.  When the request event cannot be deposited the exchange's socket\n   is closed with no synthesized response and `{:http/status :http/gone}` is\n   answered.\"\n  [boundary request socket]\n  (cond\n    (> (body-size (:body request)) (:max-body-bytes boundary))\n    {:http/status :http/too-large}\n\n    :else\n    (let [outcome (volatile! nil)\n          id (volatile! nil)]\n      (swap! (:state boundary)\n             (fn [s]\n               (cond\n                 (:stopped? s) (do (vreset! outcome :http/stopped) s)\n                 (>= (count (:open s)) (:max-open boundary))\n                 (do (vreset! outcome :http/full) s)\n                 :else\n                 (let [n (inc (:n s))\n                       xid [(:incarnation boundary) n]]\n                   (vreset! outcome :http/accepted)\n                   (vreset! id xid)\n                   (-> s\n                       (assoc :n n)\n                       (assoc-in [:open xid] {:socket socket :phase :new\n                                              :requested? false})\n                       (assoc-in [:awaiting xid] nil))))))\n      (if-not (= :http/accepted @outcome)\n        {:http/status @outcome}\n        (let [xid @id\n              ok? (deposit! boundary (request-event xid request))]\n          (if-not ok?\n            ;; Nowhere to land: the terminal event has none either.\n            (let [entry (claim! boundary xid)]\n              (swap! (:state boundary) update :awaiting dissoc xid)\n              (when entry (drop-socket! entry))\n              {:http/status :http/gone :http/exchange xid})\n            (let [parked (volatile! nil)]\n              (swap! (:state boundary)\n                     (fn [s]\n                       (vreset! parked (get-in s [:awaiting xid]))\n                       (cond-> (update s :awaiting dissoc xid)\n                         (contains? (:open s) xid)\n                         (assoc-in [:open xid :requested?] true))))\n              (when-let [event @parked]\n                (deposit! boundary (terminal-event xid event)))\n              {:http/status :http/accepted :http/exchange xid})))))))\n\n\n(defn peer-closed!\n  \"Adapter entry: the peer (or the connection) went away.  Deposits the\n   terminal `:http/closed` once; a no-op for an exchange no longer open.\"\n  [boundary id]\n  (close-exchange boundary id))\n\n\n;; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n;; Effects out\n;; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n(defn- effect?\n  [v]\n  (and (map? v) (contains? v :http/exchange) (keyword? (:http/effect v))))\n\n\n(defn- chunk-payload?\n  [x]\n  (or (string? x) (bytes/bytes? x)))\n\n\n(defn- perform!\n  \"Perform one effect for an exchange this boundary holds open.\"\n  [boundary id entry effect]\n  (let [socket (:socket entry)\n        phase (:phase entry)\n        fail! (fn [] (when-let [e (claim! boundary id)] (close-claimed! boundary id e)))\n        set-phase! (fn [p] (swap! (:state boundary)\n                                  (fn [s] (if (contains? (:open s) id)\n                                            (assoc-in s [:open id :phase] p)\n                                            s))))]\n    (case (:http/effect effect)\n      :http/head\n      (if (and (= :new phase) (integer? (:http/status effect)))\n        (if (seam-ok? (:head! socket) (:http/status effect) (or (:http/headers effect) {}))\n          (set-phase! :streaming)\n          (fail!))\n        (fail!))\n\n      :http/chunk\n      (if (and (= :streaming phase) (chunk-payload? (:http/body effect)))\n        (when-not (seam-ok? (:write! socket) (:http/body effect))\n          (fail!))\n        (fail!))\n\n      :http/end\n      (if (= :streaming phase)\n        (when-let [e (claim! boundary id)]\n          (if (seam-ok? (:end! socket))\n            (finish! boundary id e :http/ended)\n            (close-claimed! boundary id e)))\n        (fail!))\n\n      ;; An effect kind this interpreter does not know is not its own.\n      nil)))\n\n\n(defn- stop-listener!\n  [boundary]\n  (when-let [f (:on-stop @(:state boundary))]\n    (try (f) (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Throwable :cljs :default) _ nil))))\n\n\n(defn stop!\n  \"Boundary stop: close every open exchange (each deposits `:http/closed`),\n   refuse further requests, and stop the listener.  Idempotent.\"\n  [boundary]\n  (swap! (:state boundary) assoc :stopped? true)\n  (close-all! boundary)\n  (stop-listener! boundary)\n  boundary)\n\n\n(defn on-stop!\n  \"Register the host listener's stop function, run when the boundary stops or\n   its egress medium ends.\"\n  [boundary f]\n  (swap! (:state boundary) assoc :on-stop f)\n  boundary)\n\n\n(defn egress-step\n  \"Perform the effects the egress medium holds, in its order, until it has\n   none more; never waits.  Answers the boundary.\"\n  [boundary]\n  (loop []\n    (let [s @(:state boundary)]\n      (when-not (:stopped? s)\n        (let [r (stream/next (:egress boundary) (:cursor s))\n              outcome (:dao.stream/outcome r)]\n          (condp = outcome\n            :dao.stream/ok\n            (do (swap! (:state boundary) assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n                (let [v (:dao.stream/value r)]\n                  (when (effect? v)\n                    (let [id (:http/exchange v)]\n                      (when-let [entry (get-in @(:state boundary) [:open id])]\n                        (perform! boundary id entry v)))))\n                (recur))\n\n            :dao.stream/blocked nil\n\n            ;; The step cannot say whose effects were lost.\n            :dao.stream/gap\n            (do (close-all! boundary)\n                (swap! (:state boundary) assoc :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor r))\n                (recur))\n\n            ;; end, transport-error, or anything else: it can never answer again.\n            (stop! boundary))))))\n  boundary)\n(ns dao.stream.http.jvm\n  \"The JVM host edge for the DaoStream HTTP boundary (http-kit).\n\n   Wire framing, header parsing and size limits live here, below the\n   transform: an over-limit or malformed request is answered 400, 413 or 503\n   and never deposited.  The listener holds each socket open, performs the\n   effects the core asks of it by writing to the socket, and records no role\n   and reads no path, token, query or body content.\"\n  (:require [dao.stream.http :as http]\n            [org.httpkit.server :as kit])\n  (:import [java.io InputStream]))\n\n\n(defn- read-body\n  \"The request body bytes http-kit already bounded by `:max-body`, or nil.\"\n  [body]\n  (when (instance? InputStream body)\n    (let [bs (.readAllBytes ^InputStream body)]\n      (when (pos? (alength bs)) bs))))\n\n\n(defn- socket\n  \"The core's socket seam over one http-kit async channel.  `kit/send!`\n   answers false once the channel is closed, which the core reads as a failed\n   write.\"\n  [ch]\n  {:head! (fn [status headers]\n            (kit/send! ch {:status status :headers headers} false))\n   :write! (fn [chunk] (kit/send! ch chunk false))\n   :end! (fn [] (kit/close ch) true)\n   :close! (fn [] (kit/close ch) true)})\n\n\n(defn- answer!\n  [ch status]\n  (kit/send! ch {:status status :headers {\"content-type\" \"text/plain\"}} true))\n\n\n(defn- handler\n  [boundary]\n  (fn [req]\n    (let [exchange (atom nil)]\n      (kit/as-channel\n        req\n        {:on-open (fn [ch]\n                    (try\n                      (let [result (http/accept!\n                                     boundary\n                                     {:method (:request-method req)\n                                      :path (:uri req)\n                                      :query (:query-string req)\n                                      :headers (:headers req)\n                                      :body (read-body (:body req))}\n                                     (socket ch))]\n                        (case (:http/status result)\n                          :http/accepted (reset! exchange (:http/exchange result))\n                          :http/too-large (answer! ch 413)\n                          (:http/full :http/stopped) (answer! ch 503)\n                          :http/gone nil))\n                      (catch Throwable _ (answer! ch 400))))\n         :on-close (fn [_ch _status]\n                     (when-let [id @exchange]\n                       (http/peer-closed! boundary id)))}))))\n\n\n(defn listen!\n  \"Bind an http-kit listener for `boundary`.  Options: `:bind-host`,\n   `:bind-port` (0 for ephemeral).  The boundary's `:max-body-bytes` bounds\n   http-kit's own body read, so an oversize request is answered 413 before it\n   is assembled.  Registers the listener's stop with the boundary and answers\n   `{:http.jvm/server server :http.jvm/port port}`.\"\n  [boundary {:keys [bind-host bind-port]}]\n  (let [server (kit/run-server (handler boundary)\n                               {:ip bind-host\n                                :port bind-port\n                                :max-body (:max-body-bytes boundary)\n                                :legacy-return-value? false})]\n    (http/on-stop! boundary #(kit/server-stop! server {:timeout 100}))\n    {:http.jvm/server server\n     :http.jvm/port (kit/server-port server)}))\n\n\n(defn stop-listening!\n  [{:http.jvm/keys [server]}]\n  (kit/server-stop! server {:timeout 100}))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/design/dao.lease.md; sed -n '95,165p' docs/design/dao.star.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat docs/design/dao.lease.md; sed -n '95,165p' docs/design/dao.star.md\"","aggregated_output":"# DaoLease\n\nStatus: implemented (`src/cljc/dao/lease.cljc`, `test/dao/lease_test.cljc`,\n`test/dao/lease_composition_test.cljc`; merged 2026-09-20), derived from and\nsubordinate to [`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). The three use cases in\n[`dao.lease.implementation-plan.md`](./dao.lease.implementation-plan.md) are\nsketches, not finished components. This document is the operative contract:\nevery sentence below is a rule. Why the design is this shape is in\n[`dao.lease.rationale.md`](./dao.lease.rationale.md), which binds nothing.\n\nA lease is a grant that lapses unless renewed. It adds no operation to any\ncontract and no key to any DaoStream result map, leaving\n[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) unchanged by its existence.\n\n## Vocabulary\n\nFacts are plain data on ordinary streams, classified by two dispatch keys:\n`:dao.lease/status` for the negotiation, `:dao.lease/event` for evidence. A\nreader switches on both. A fact carrying neither is not a lease fact and is\nignored; a fact carrying both is defective.\n\n+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Fact     | `:dao.lease/status`   | Author  | Identity carried                            | Also required                                                             |\n+==========+=======================+=========+=============================================+===========================================================================+\n| Proposal | `:dao.lease/proposed` | holder  | `:dao.lease/proposal`                       | `:dao.lease/subject`; `:dao.lease/duration` optional, an ask              |\n+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Grant    | `:dao.lease/accepted` | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`, plus                    | `:dao.lease/subject`, `:dao.lease/holder`, `:dao.lease/duration`;         |\n|          |                       |         | `:dao.lease/proposal` if answering one      | `:dao.lease/max` optional                                                 |\n+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Refusal  | `:dao.lease/rejected` | grantor | `:dao.lease/proposal`                       | —                                                                         |\n+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Release  | `:dao.lease/released` | holder  | `:dao.lease/lease`                          | —                                                                         |\n+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n| Reclaim  | `:dao.lease/lapsed`   | grantor | `:dao.lease/lease`                          | `:dao.lease/cause` — `:silence`, `:release`, `:cap`, or `:policy`         |\n+----------+-----------------------+---------+---------------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n\n+------------+----------------------+---------+----------------------+----------------------+\n| Evidence   | `:dao.lease/event`   | Author  | Identity carried     | Also required        |\n+============+======================+=========+======================+======================+\n| Renewal    | `:dao.lease/renewal` | holder  | `:dao.lease/lease`   | —                    |\n+------------+----------------------+---------+----------------------+----------------------+\n| Tick       | `:dao.lease/tick`    | adapter | —                    | `:dao.lease/reading` |\n+------------+----------------------+---------+----------------------+----------------------+\n\n**Keys.**\n\n- `:dao.lease/lease` — the lease's identity, minted by the grantor, carried by\n  every fact about it, never reused within that grantor.\n- `:dao.lease/proposal` — a proposal's identity, minted by the holder, echoed\n  by the grant or refusal answering it.\n- `:dao.lease/subject` — what is leased, in whatever plain data the domain uses.\n- `:dao.lease/holder` — the party whose renewals count, in whatever form the\n  medium's attribution takes.\n- `:dao.lease/duration` — a single-entry map from a unit keyword to a positive\n  integer magnitude. `:dao.lease/max`, `:dao.lease/reading` and a judge's\n  tolerance use the same representation; tolerance may be zero. A magnitude\n  stays within the bound that keeps every comparison and sum of the\n  composition's units exact on every host — the reference bound is 2^52 raw\n  and, against a supplied table, `quot 2^52 unit-magnitude`; a magnitude past\n  it establishes nothing.\n- `:dao.lease/cause` — why a reclaim happened.\n\n**Units.** The unit keywords and their ratios are a composition's\ninteroperability decision, fixed once and shared by its grantor, holders and\njudge. Comparison normalizes to the finer of two units, and is strict: an\ninterval exactly equal to its bound has not yet passed it.\n\n**Validity.** A fact is defective, and establishes nothing, when it omits a key\nits status or event requires; carries a status or event outside the tables\nabove; carries a duration, cap or reading that is not a single-entry map of a\nfixed unit to a positive integer; carries a lease id on a proposal; answers one\nproposal with both `:accepted` and `:rejected`, or repeats `:accepted`,\n`:released` or `:lapsed` for one lease; or carries a reading older than one\nalready observed. A judge reading a defective fact has read no lease fact.\n\n## Authority\n\n- **Only grantor-authored facts establish terms.** A holder cannot grant itself\n  tenure. A holder fact establishes no term; a delayed holder renewal is still\n  evidence and counts as such.\n- **A renewal counts only when its attributed author is the lease's holder.**\n  A renewal from any other author is a fact about that author, and silence is\n  measured as though it had not arrived.\n- **At most one of `:accepted` or `:rejected` answers a proposal.**\n  Renegotiating is a new proposal with a new proposal identity.\n- **A proposal creates no state.** Nothing exists until `:accepted`. A grantor\n  owes no answer, and a holder's wait on an unanswered proposal is bounded by\n  nothing in this vocabulary.\n- **A grantor may grant unsolicited**, with no proposal, in which case the\n  holder is the party the grant is delivered to.\n- **A `:policy` cause may not consist of declining to count evidence\n  received.** Eligible renewals are counted whether or not another condition\n  ends the lease.\n\n## Prohibitions\n\n- A lease may not extend retention, defer eviction, or gate history, and no\n  DaoStream operation may consult a lease.\n- No timer holds a callback and no notification marks a lapse.\n- No absolute time appears in any fact.\n- A reclaim frees the resource, never the record.\n- A holder renews from its own control flow. No machinery renews on its behalf.\n\n## Time\n\nTime reaches lease code only as data; neither judge nor holder reads a host\nclock. Both read a tick stream their composition wires. Readings on one stream\nnever decrease, and a composition persisting a ledger across a restart keeps\nits readings comparable across it.\n\nA tick-stream gap makes a pass late, not wrong; a lease-fact gap is governed\nunder *Judging with incomplete evidence*.\n\n## The judge\n\nThe judge is composed **inside the boundary that possesses the resource** and\nreclaims by acting on what it itself holds; one that does not possess the\nresource is outside this contract. It concludes and reclaims in one\ninterpreter, which is not the forbidden collapse: it is the possessing host\ninterpreter performing its own act.\n\n**Ledger.** The judge keeps, privately, for each live lease:\n\n- its **terms** — subject, holder, duration, cap;\n- its **tenure start**, the reading the grant was seeded at, which no renewal\n  changes;\n- its **last relevant observation**, the reading of the grant or newest\n  eligible renewal, whichever is later;\n- its **evidence state** — `known`, or `unknown` with its resumed reading;\n- its **reclaim state** — `live`, or `pending` carrying the classified cause\n  and whether the reclaim succeeded.\n\nA lease **enters** the ledger when the grantor grants it, seeded from that act,\nand **leaves** once its reclaim has succeeded and its `:lapsed` record has been\nappended with `:dao.stream/ok`. Until both, it remains.\n\nThe ledger is not rebuildable from any stream: a composition either persists it,\nas a stream its owner writes and reads, or recovers by the rule under *Restart*.\n\n### The pass\n\nA pass classifies nothing until a first tick has been observed. At each pass,\nin this order:\n\n1. **Drain every wired tick cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`. The newest\n   reading drained, or the newest previously observed if none were drained, is\n   **now** for the whole pass.\n2. **Drain every wired lease-fact cursor** to `:dao.stream/blocked`, stamping\n   each fact with that same *now*, and apply it: an eligible renewal advances\n   the lease's last relevant observation to *now* and returns it to `known`; a\n   valid `:released` from the holder marks it released; a `:dao.stream/gap`\n   marks every lease on that medium `unknown` with *now* as its resumed\n   reading. Each cursor's drain may be bounded by a composition-declared\n   budget; a cursor still yielding when the budget is spent has not completed\n   its observation, and while any wired fact cursor is so truncated, the pass\n   classifies no `:silence` for the leases that truncated medium carries —\n   `:release`, `:cap`, `:policy` and pending retries are never suppressed. An\n   incomplete drain may delay a reclaim; it may never manufacture one.\n3. **Answer and grant.** Proposals drained this pass may be answered, and\n   unsolicited grants authored, both seeded and stamped with this pass's *now*.\n   A grant authored between passes is seeded with the next pass's *now*.\n4. **Classify.** A lease is due for reclaim when any of these holds, and the\n   first that holds in this order is its cause: it is already `pending`, and\n   keeps the cause it carries; a valid `:released` from its holder has been\n   observed, `:release`; its tenure start is further back than\n   `:dao.lease/max`, `:cap`; the grantor's own policy ends it, `:policy`; its\n   interval since the last relevant observation exceeds duration plus\n   tolerance, `:silence`. A lease whose evidence state is `unknown` is due for\n   `:silence` only once, in addition, a full duration has passed since its\n   resumed reading.\n5. **Reclaim** each due lease by its composition-supplied procedure, marking it\n   `pending` with its cause first. The procedure is idempotent and reports\n   whether it succeeded; a reclaim that fails or does not report leaves the\n   lease `pending` and unrecorded.\n6. **Record** `:lapsed`, carrying the cause the ledger holds, for each lease\n   whose reclaim succeeded. On `:dao.stream/ok` the lease leaves the ledger;\n   otherwise it stays `pending` and the next pass reaches it again through the\n   first clause of step 4.\n\nThe record follows the act and never precedes it. **The absence of a `:lapsed`\nfact is never evidence of tenure.**\n\nA drain that ends in `:dao.stream/end` retires that cursor for subsequent\npasses. A drain that ends in `:dao.stream/transport-error` ends the pass before\nstep 4.\n\n### Judging with incomplete evidence\n\nAbsence is evidence only over a window the judge observed. After a\n`:dao.stream/gap`, a lease with no renewal in the retained tail is `unknown`,\nand a surviving older renewal is not inferred to be the newest. A lease\nrecovered from a persisted ledger is `unknown`. Conditions other than\n`:silence` apply to an `unknown` lease unchanged.\n\n### Restart\n\nA grantor that has lost its ledger **reclaims and re-grants**: for each\nresource it still possesses it performs the reclaim first, then grants afresh.\nThe reclaim ends the prior tenure; a new grant alone does not.\n\nThis requires an inventory of possessed resources and a recoverable holder for\neach, both of which belong to the resource rather than to this vocabulary.\nA grantor with neither persists its ledger instead.\n\n## The holder\n\n- **Observe the grant before acting**, and establish that the grantor authored\n  it by the same attribution its composition supplies to the judge. A holder\n  that has not observed its grant holds nothing.\n- **Renew at less than half the duration**, measured against ticks on its own\n  tick stream. A renewal is an append returning `:dao.stream/ok`; any other\n  outcome does not advance the holder's bound.\n- **Stop acting at the bound** — the earlier of the duration since the later of\n  its last renewal and its observed grant, and the cap the grant carries —\n  whether or not anything has been heard.\n- **Release when done.** The grantor still performs the reclaim and records\n  it.\n- **Treat an unusable reading as unanswerable.** A reading that is not a\n  single-entry `{unit, positive integer magnitude}` within the composition's\n  table answers nothing: every holder discipline fails closed on it, and a\n  flow that guards on its bound alone does not act.\n\nFor cap purposes, the holder measures the cap interval from the reading at\nwhich it observed the grant; that reading may be later than the grantor\njudge's tenure start. The holder's observation-flight interval does not\nextend, delay, or alter the judge's cap or reclaim decision; a resource\nrequiring exclusion must enforce it through fencing.\n\nThe bound bounds attention, not access; a holder needing exclusion obtains it\nfrom the resource.\n\n## Composition duties\n\nA composition that grants leases owes all of the following.\n\n- **A judging interpreter, and a runtime driving it at a declared cadence** —\n  the maximum interval between *completed* passes.\n- **A tick stream** for the judge, and one for every holder.\n- **A tolerance** supplied to the judge, possibly zero.\n- **An attribution resolver** for the judge and for every holder observing a\n  grant — its own answer to who authored a fact, by per-author media, an\n  envelope key, or a transport's attachment identity, since DaoStream supplies\n  none. One handed no resolver, or one incompatible with its medium, is refused\n  at assembly.\n- **A reclaim procedure per subject**, idempotent, reporting success.\n- **Over a durable ledger, a reclaim that only reports readiness.** The\n  revocation is the transaction that records `:lapsed` and its accompanying\n  facts; its commit is the linearization point. No state claiming a\n  successful revocation leaves the grantor's boundary before that commit. A\n  grantor stream that does not answer `:dao.stream/ok` leaves the lease\n  `pending` in the judge and the recorded state unchanged. A judge rebuilt\n  at restart drains its proposal media from their oldest anchor, so a\n  proposal drained but left unanswered before the restart is judged again.\n- **A stream the grantor writes grants and `:lapsed` to**, and the medium each\n  recipient reads its carried facts from.\n- **Media that either retain without eviction or declare evict-oldest**,\n  refused at assembly otherwise.\n- **For durable resources, three things this vocabulary does not supply**: a\n  judge whose durability matches the resource, a rule for which incarnation may\n  reclaim, and fencing from the resource. A composition that has not settled\n  all three keeps its leases process-scoped.\n\n## Sizing\n\nStated as relations; the values belong to compositions.\n\n- The granted duration exceeds twice the holder's renewal interval.\n- The judge's tolerance covers expected flight time and the rate skew between\n  its tick stream and the holder's.\n- A medium's retention window exceeds the judge's lag — cadence plus drain time.\n- A cap below the duration is permitted, and tenure then ends at the cap.\n- The holder's renewal interval plus one period of its tick stream is\n  strictly below half the granted duration: a renewal lands at the first\n  reading at or past the interval, up to one period late, so the\n  before-half guarantee is bought against interval plus period.\n\n## Limits\n\n- **A false lapse is possible.** Delay errs against the holder while a renewal\n  is unobserved and for it once observed, so a late-observed renewal from a\n  dead holder buys a fresh duration, and continuity is lost when none is\n  observed before a completed pass finds the interval past duration plus\n  tolerance. Partition length alone decides neither.\n- **`:dao.lease/max` binds within one ledger lifetime.**\n- **A medium that gaps more often than one duration** never completes an\n  observation window and leaks continuously while every component reports\n  healthy; per-attachment media are the isolation.\n- **Reclamation is bounded by** duration plus tolerance plus cadence plus the\n  reclaim's own cost, given an uninterrupted observation window.\n\n## Carriage\n\nProposals, grants, refusals, releases and renewals cross a boundary as ordinary\npayload, carried by the composition to the medium the recipient reads. Carriage\nis delivery, not a second authoring.\n\nA renewal, like a proposal and a release, may be carried by the\nauthority's front under the holder's request identity, with the same\nresolver attribution and the same carriage as the proposal and\nrelease it accompanies (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 14.2.4). The judge\ncounts the carried renewal; carriage adds no key to any outcome map.\n\n`:lapsed` does not cross; it is the grantor's record on the grantor's stream. A\nremote holder learns of a reclaim by observing it — for a served connection, an\nordinary `:ws/closed`; for a stream served under `dao.stream.remote.md`, the\n`not-found` protocol error its mirror answers for the reclaimed identity. A remote holder that\nreads the grantor's stream through a served handle is observing the grantor's\nrecord, not receiving carriage. No close code is assigned here; distinguishing\nreclaim on the wire belongs to `dao.stream.ws.md`'s deferred close-code design.\n\n## Out of scope\n\n- **Delegated renewal.** A renewal is evidence about its author.\n- **Transfer of a lease between holders.**\n- **Who may be granted anything at all**, and **what is leasable** — this\n  contract gates nothing, and the domain decides. `dao.stream.remote.md`\n  names two subjects: a stream entry served for a remote party, and a relay\n  pair on a meeting peer.\n\n**Keep-alive is the responder's.** On its tick it may append an `:http/chunk` carrying an SSE comment line. That chunk is bytes for the wire, so the host discovers a dead peer by writing; the renewal is evidence for the judge. Neither stands in for the other. A host whose send buffers (http-kit) may not report a peer that disconnects while an exchange is idle; the exchange lapses by `:silence` and is closed by reclaim, with latency bounded by the lease duration plus judge cadence. A peer's close or a failed write is deposited as `:http/closed`; the responder reads it as data and drops the exchange, and the judge ends the exchange lease with cause `:policy`.\n\n**The lineage is leased** (owner ruling 2026-10-07; the holder choice below is the architect's). A lineage nobody returns to is reclaimed through `dao.lease.md`, not by a timer. The peer holding the parked record is the grantor. When a lineage begins, its judge grants unsolicited on the lease medium: subject and holder `{:dao.star/lineage S}`, duration the composition's idle allowance, `:dao.lease/max` optional. Attribution is by envelope key: the session-facts medium is written only by session drivers, each stamping `:dao.star/lineage` on its facts, renewals and release, and the judge's resolver reads that key; until ShiBi, any writer of that medium can renew, the same exposure the exchange lease has. The driver observes its grant before consuming or projecting, renews at under half the duration from its own control flow, on each consumed input and on its tick while it holds an open subscription, and **stops at its bound**: past it, it consumes nothing and projects nothing, and an input arriving then is handed to the root program as begin. So a connected session is retained for as long as it stays connected, by design, bounded only by the cap; a disconnected one lapses after the idle allowance. The lease ends with the lineage: released by the driver on halt, lapsed by the judge on silence. Reclaim retires the runnable resource, the current parked record; the `:lapsed` record on the lease medium is the judge's own and is what ends the lineage for the router. History, successor facts and lease records are kept (`dao.lease.md`: a reclaim frees the resource, never the record). The subject is distinct from Mode 2's custody subject `{:yin.k/occurrence O}` on the arbitration medium. Leases are process-scoped until the composition settles the three durable duties `dao.lease.md` names.\n\nSizing: duration exceeds twice the renewal interval; the keep-alive interval stays under the shortest proxy or NAT idle timeout on the path; the ingress window exceeds router and judge lag and each session medium's window exceeds its driver's; a boundary restart loses sockets and ledger together, so its exchange inventory to reclaim is empty. On hosts whose sends buffer, the exchange lease's duration is also the dead-peer detection bound. A false lapse is possible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\n\n---\n\n## Mode 2: The Fat Client (Mobile Continuations via UCF)\n\nStatus: proposed. Everything in this section waits on UCF version 1, fenced custody with a `dao.space` transactor as arbitration authority (`yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` §7.7, 7.7.8). Version 0 is fork-only and cannot offer the exclusivity below.\n\nWhile `dao.star` champions the dumb terminal, it is not locked into it. Because `yin.vm` continuations are pure data, they can cross the network boundary to act as SPA-style fat clients for low-latency local interaction.\n\n### Flow\n\n1. **Exporting**: A `yin.vm` continuation on the server parks and enters the exporting state (UCF §7.7.4): its wait set is detached, so no poll or local resume can perform an effect while the value is in other hands. The lift produces a UCF handoff body, canonical and content-addressed, publishes it (*Handoff carrier*), and the driver records the offer on the arbitration medium.\n2. **Handoff**: The SSE patch carries the notice, not the body. A browser-side adapter deposits the notice on a browser-local stream that the browser's `yin.vm` reads; Datastar hands nothing to anyone.\n3. **Custody**: The browser `yin.vm` fetches the body by address over its WebSocket channel, proposes on the arbitration medium, and resumes only on observing its grant. Custody is exclusive because the lease is: the state moves to the browser rather than being shared with it, and the server's copy stays fenced.\n4. **Transparent Remote Access**: A continuation that needs a server-held stream uses the portable descriptors it carried and `attach!`es a reflection over WebSockets. Descriptors carry no authorization: the served entries are gated and leased for the migrated continuation (`dao.stream.remote.md`, sections 6 and 7). On channel loss a reflection answers `channel-gone` and reattachment is the continuation's own step on the same descriptor; a reclaimed entry answers `not-found` and is gone, not disconnected.\n5. **Migration Back**: The continuation re-serializes itself as a successor and offers it to the server, which admits it under *Admitting a continuation back*.\n\n### Handoff carrier\n\n**Architect ruling, not yet confirmed by the owner (2026-10-07).** The SSE patch carries a notice and the body travels by address over the browser's WebSocket channel. The notice is the body's content address, the arbitration descriptor, and a content coordinate `{:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/remote :dao.jing/requests <remote descriptor> :dao.jing/answers <remote descriptor>}` (`dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md`, section 1).\n\nThe path: before the notice is appended, the lift publishes the body to the server's content store and appends the address to the session-facts medium as the lineage's export. The `requests` and `answers` entries are a pair served per lineage, so answers are not readable by other lineages, and gated with the lineage token as the pre-ShiBi credential; the gate's `verify` admits a `get` only for an address the lineage's own export facts name. The browser's fetch is the stepped content client, clock-free; it verifies the body by re-hashing (UCF §7.8, lower step 2). Three absences are distinct and all arrive as data: missing content is the service's `:jing/found? false`, a missing service is the remote `not-found` error, and a disallowed address is `:dao.stream/refused` at the gate. In each the browser holds nothing, and the exporter, a candidate like any other, proposes for itself under its own policy (UCF §7.7.4), so an unfetched offer strands nothing. Confidentiality is the gate's and the pair's: an address is a name, not an authorization.\n\nReasons: the body is canonical CBOR and SSE is text, so inlining means a second encoding; the egress medium is shared, and a body on it discloses the store slice to every observer, where a notice discloses a name; large values travel as content addresses (`dao.stream.md`, Granularity); and the browser `yin.vm` needs the channel anyway for custody and remote access, so the carrier adds no path.\n\nRejected: the body base64-encoded inside a `datastar-patch-signals` or element attribute (double encoding, disclosure on egress, Datastar's payload size); a second HTTP GET by address (a second boundary path for what the channel already carries).\n\n### Admitting a continuation back\n\nA continuation returning from the browser is admitted as untrusted code and state. It ran where nothing can attest to it, so it is treated as the controlled mode of `dao.space.security.md` treats any pushed interpreter.\n\n1. **Custody first** (UCF §7.7, unchanged). The successor must name, in `:yin.k/origin`, the lease the ledger holds live for that holder, and arrive on the holder's own medium. Otherwise `:yin.k/not-holder`.\n2. **Validate as hostile bytes** (UCF §7.8, lower steps 1 to 4), under a size bound and a step budget the composition declares.\n3. **Rebind authority; never read it from the value.** Lower into an isolated store. Primitives and FFI ops resolve from the receiver's registry, restricted to the set the composition delegated to this lineage at export. A stream marker attaches only if its identity is in that delegated set, or its channel is the returning peer's own. Anything else is `:yin.k/unsatisfied` naming it.\n4. **No check survives the trip.** A decision the program made before export may have been skipped by a forged frame. Whatever must hold after return is enforced by a gate on a stream, or stays home in a continuation that never left and answers the migrated one over streams.\n\nThe test: a returned continuation can do nothing its principal could not do directly through the gated streams it was given.\n\n**Export is disclosure.** The lifted store slice and values are readable by the browser and by every peer the content gate admits. The composition inspects the computed closure before publishing.\n\n**Until effect handlers honor a delegated set** (`dao.space.security.md`, Honest Limits), migration back is admitted from allow-listed peers only (owner ruling 2026-10-07). ShiBi later plugs in at the same gates.\n\n---\n\n## Design Invariants Maintained\n\n- **No Shared Mutable State**: The UI is a pure projection (Mode 1), or custody of the continuation is held under an exclusive lease (Mode 2). State is never shared across the boundary; it moves, and the mover is fenced.\n- **Multiplexed Correlation**: Connections are not mapped 1:1 to streams. The HTTP boundary observes a shared egress stream and routes by exchange id. The cost is shared: an egress `gap` closes every open exchange, and a composition under that pressure shards the egress.\n- **Host Isolation**: The HTTP boundary is a dumb pipe that knows nothing of Datastar or `yin.vm` syntax. Datastar is a dumb renderer that knows nothing of backend state. Under Mode 2 a `yin.vm` runs beside it, not inside it.\n- **Data > Macros**: Compile-time slicing (Electric Clojure) is rejected. Network boundaries are crossed dynamically at runtime via reified data on explicit streams.\n- **No callbacks, no implicit control flow**: every arrival is a deposit, every departure is an effect, and every wait is a `blocked` answer at a cursor.\n- **No hidden state**: a driver's open subscriptions and the router's token index are interpreter state derived from media each reads with its own cursor, rebuilt from those media or honestly lost on a gap.\n\n## Deferred\n\n- **Multi-shot forks** (Arc's back button resuming a sibling branch): branch identity and subscription routing with a non-unique current are unspecified.\n- **Local projection** in Mode 2: the browser `yin.vm` appends the same Datastar-format effects to a browser-local egress stream read by a browser-side boundary adapter; same vocabulary, different boundary.\n- **Compaction of the session-facts medium**: waits on `dao.stream.md` OD-4.\n- **The content service as rules**: `dao.jing.content` is a plan section, not a spec; the notice above depends on it.\n- **An HTTP channel for peers without WebSocket**: `dao.stream.http.md`, Deferred.\n\n## Completion Criteria\n\n1. **Thin Client**: A `yin.vm` agent receives a Datastar input through the HTTP boundary's ingress stream, answers with `:http/head`, SSE-formatted `:http/chunk`s and `:http/end` on the egress stream, parks, and resumes on the next input for its current token. Also: a request with no token begins a lineage; a subscribe consumes nothing and a re-subscribe after several inputs receives the current projection; a stale token is re-projected; a closed socket is observed as `:http/closed`; a silent exchange lapses and closes; a lineage nobody returns to lapses, its parked record is retired and its facts remain; a request naming a just-published token is never answered as begin; after an ingress gap reconciliation leaves no exchange unleased and no closed exchange leased, and a driver stops renewing an exchange once it observes the lapse or its own gap; a renewal in flight may still count while the lease is live, and one observed after retirement does not revive it.\n2. **Fat Client Handoff**, conditional on UCF version 1 landing: a `yin.vm` agent exports under exclusive custody, the SSE patch carries the notice, a CLJS `yin.vm` in the browser fetches the body through the lineage's gated content pair, verifies it, obtains the grant, resumes, and reaches a server-held stream through a gated, leased reflection. Migration back from an allow-listed peer is admitted under *Admitting a continuation back*. The carrier shape is an architect ruling pending the owner's confirmation.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
